# Given: a keyring holding an AES-256 key, a SECRET and an 8 byte AES entry
# When: an absent id, the SECRET and the odd-length entry are each used
# Then: the same "no usable AES key" error for all three (errors section)
# Scenario 2 — Given: ids that are not ids
# When: an empty id, a 256 byte id and an id with a NUL byte are used
# Then: an invalid-id error for each (errors section)
# Scenario 3 — Given: the longest id allowed, with nothing stored under it
# When: it is used
# Then: it is a valid id, so the answer is "no usable AES key" (errors section)
# Scenario 4 — Given: NULL in each argument position
# When: the functions are called
1	1	1	1
# Then: NULL each time, even under an id that names nothing (above)
# Scenario 5 — Given: a table whose rows name two different key ids
# When: the id is taken from the column
# Then: refused on the second id — one key id per call site per statement (errors section)
# Scenario 6 — Given: a table whose rows all name the same key id
# When: the id is taken from the column
3
# Then: three rows sealed — the rule is about the bytes, not about being a constant (above)
# Scenario 7 — Given: an envelope with one bit of its last tag byte flipped, strict at its default
# When: it is opened by key id
# Then: a tag error, never NULL and never plaintext (errors section)
# Scenario 8 — Given: an AES-128 envelope and the id of an AES-256 key
# When: the envelope is opened under that id
# Then: a suite mismatch that does not state the key length (errors section)
# Scenario 9 — Given: the key id sent as a bound parameter
# When: the statements are executed
홍길동
# Then: the name — a parameter is not visible in init, so the key is read on the first row (above)
# Scenario 13 — Given: a usable key, and envelopes that are malformed rather than unauthentic
# When: each is opened by key id
# Then: each is an envelope error carrying the envelope length and nothing about the key (errors section)
# Scenario 10 — Given: the three functions
# When: each is called with one argument and with four
# Then: each is refused when the statement is prepared, with its usage (errors section)
# Scenario 11 — Given: three sealed rows, the middle one with a bit of its tag flipped, and gcm.strict OFF
# When: one statement opens all three by key id
1	김철수
2	NULL
3	박길수
# Then: name, NULL, name — the key read on the first row survives the bad tag (above)
# Scenario 12 — Given: gcm.strict still OFF
# When: the rows are opened under an id that names nothing
# Then: an error, not NULL — a key that cannot be had is not hidden by the setting (errors section)
